      "data-passage": "chapter-38-p-85f677e565",
      children: "This quiet interregnum deserves a moment's attention, because the Bayesian methods that flourished in the 1990s were the direct descendants of Ramsey's 1926 insight. Start with degrees of belief. Observe data. Update by Bayes's rule. The prior washes out as evidence accumulates. The Bayesian machine learning community — David MacKay, Radford Neal, Michael Jordan — built models that combined structured assumptions with probabilistic learning, exactly the fusion Ramsey had formalized. They didn't scale. They needed too much computation per inference. But they were the most principled learners anyone had built, and their framework — priors, likelihoods, posteriors — would quietly infiltrate the deep learning revolution when it came. Variational autoencoders, Bayesian neural networks, uncertainty estimation: the optimization tradition kept rediscovering that it needed probability, just as Ramsey had shown it would."
